      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i'll look into a soil test. what kind of ph range am i aiming for for fescue? and if the test shows my ph is way off, does that mean the phosphorus is just stuck in the dirt or what? i'm just trying to understand the science of why it's not working",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For most fescue varieties, you want to aim for a slightly acidic to neutral pH range, ideally between 6.0 and 7.0. If your soil pH is significantly off—say, too acidic or too alkaline—it can absolutely lock up nutrients, making phosphorus effectively unavailable to your grass roots, even if it's physically present in the soil. This is a common issue, and it's not that the phosphorus is gone; it's just bound up in a chemical form the fescue can't \"drink.\" You can use soil amendments like lime to raise pH or sulfur to lower it, depending on your test results.",
